%[text]  You can use numbers, `pi`, `sqrt()`, and the trigonometric functions `sin()`, cos(), `atan()`, `sind()`, `cosd()`, and `atand()` in expressing your solution.
%[text] ![Warning symbol](text:image:6726) **Practical Note:** The checking code on this exercise is looking for a precise answer. If you round your solution too much, your answer will not be accepted. 
%[text] ![MATLAB icon](text:image:0679) **Pro-tip**. The functions `sin()`, cos(), and `atan()` compute with angles in radians while  `sind()`, `cosd()`, and `atand()` compute with angles in degrees.
solnPt = "(sqrt(29),atan(-5/2),-3)";  %[control:editfield:9179]{"position":[10,36]} %[control:button:2952]{"position":[37,38]}

if exist("startCoords","var") && exist("endCoords","var") %[output:group:2c74bb91]
    numPt = PullOutPoint(solnPt,endCoords,Convention);
    out = CheckExercise4(numPt,startPt,startCoords,endCoords,Convention); %[output:6158c358]
else
    warning("You must generate a problem before solving it.")
end %[output:group:2c74bb91]
